<h2>Topic 2: Utility, Risk Aversion and Portfolio Choice</h2><p><b>Estimated time:</b> ~35 minutes | <b>Objectives:</b> Explain utility, risk aversion, diversification, calculate expected return and variance.</p>
<p>People do not just want high return - they want high return for low risk. Utility measures satisfaction.</p><div class='keybox'><p><b>Utility:</b> U = E[R] - 0.5 x A x Var[R], A = risk aversion. Higher A = more risk averse.</p><p><b>Diversification:</b> If two assets do not move together, mixing them cuts risk without cutting return much.</p><p><b>Example:</b> Asset A: E=10%, SD=20%. Asset B: E=10%, SD=20%. If correlation = 0, 50/50 portfolio: E=10%, Var = 0.25x400 + 0.25x400 + 0 = 200. SD = 14.14%. Same return, lower risk.</p></div>
